2026-06-30 15:29:38,849 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-f9706fd1-e1d8-4467-bc7d-ee4f4e3fd881', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nq181030q06.raw\nq181030q10.raw\nq181030q14.raw\nq181030q18.raw\nq181130q01.raw\nq181130q04.raw\nq181130q07.raw\nq181130q10.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nCSE1L and NUP37 proximity labeling in MCF10A cells expressing chimeric ErbB1:ErbB2 receptors\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThis project uses an MCF10A clone (B2B1) expressing chimeric synthetic receptors for ErbB1 (EGFR) and ErbB2 (HER2) along with BirA*-fused inducible alleles of CSE1L or NUP37.  Cells were cultured with reconstituted basement membrane, BirA* alleles induced with doxycycline, ErbB receptors dimerized with a synthetic small molecule, and biotinylation was performed for 24 hours.  Lysates underwent a two-step biotin affinity purification before gel electrophoresis and mass spectrometry on selected gel fragments.\n\n[METHODS]\nFour 15-cm plates of B2B1 cells stably expressing inducible BirA*-CSE1L or BirA*-NUP37 were prepared as overlay cultures, induced with 1 µg/ml doxycycline (Sigma, D9891) on Day 5, heterodimerized with 0.5 µM AP21967 (Takara, 635057) on Day 6, and labeled with 50 µM biotin (Sigma, B4639) on Day 7 for 24 hours before lysis in RIPA buffer (50 mM Tris-HCl [pH 7.5], 150 mM NaCl, 1% Triton X-100, 0.5% sodium deoxycholate, 0.1% sodium dodecyl sulfate, 5 mM EDTA) plus protease and phosphatase inhibitors (10 µg/ml aprotinin, 10 µg/ml leupeptin, 1 µg/ml pepstatin, 1 mM PMSF, 200 µM Na3VO4).  Clarified lysate (3 ml) was mixed with 8 ml IAP buffer (50 mM MOPS [pH 7.2], 10 mM sodium phosphate [pH 7.4], 50 mM NaCl) and immunoprecipitated with 500 µl anti-biotin agarose (ImmuneChem, ICP0615) overnight at 4ºC.  Immunoprecipitates were washed four times with PBS plus protease and phosphatase inhibitors and eluted with 0.15% trifluoroacetic acid followed by re-equilibration to pH 7 with 1 M NaOH.  The eluate was re-purified with 50 µl streptavidin magnetic beads (Thermo Fisher, PI88816) for two hours at room temperature, washed three times with TBS + 0.1% Tween 20 plus protease and phosphatase inhibitors, and boiled in Laemmli sample buffer.  Samples were electrophoresed on a 10% polyacrylamide gel and fragments excised to avoid nonspecific bands at 57, 41, 27, and 16 kDa before mass spectrometry.  Gel fragments were transferred to a siliconized tube, washed in 200 µl 50% methanol, dehydrated in acetonitrile, rehydrated in 30 µl 10 mM dithiolthreitol in 0.1 M ammonium bicarbonate, and reduced at room temperature for 0.5 h.  Samples were alkylated in 30 µl 50 mM iodoacetamide in 0.1 M ammonium bicarbonate at room temperature for 30 minutes, dehydrated in 100 µl acetonitrile, rehydrated in 100 µl 0.1 M ammonium bicarbonate, and then dehydrated again in 100 µL acetonitrile before drying completely by vacuum centrifugation.  Gel fragments were rehydrated in 20 ng/µl trypsin in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate on ice for 30 minutes.  Excess enzyme solution was removed, 20 µl 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate added, and the sample was digested overnight at 37ºC.  Digested peptides were extracted in 100 µl 50% acetonitrile and 5% formic acid and evaporated to 15 µl for MS analysis on a Thermo Electron Q Exactive HFX mass spectrometer system with an Easy Spray ion source connected to a Thermo 75 µm x 15 cm C18 Easy Spray column.  A 4 µl or 7 µl equivalent of the extract was injected and the peptides eluted from the column by an acetonitrile/0.1 M acetic acid gradient at a flow rate of 0.3 µl/minute over one hour.  The nanospray ion source was operated at 1.9 kV.  The digest was analyzed by acquiring a full scan mass spectrum to determine peptide molecular weights followed by product ion spectra (20 HCD) to determine amino acid sequence in sequential scans.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
