2026-06-30 15:29:53,866 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-ce350128-0339-4c2b-b63e-f493eaf347ac', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to the mass spectrometry acquisition.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: instrument\nsdrf_column: comment[instrument]\nontology: MS:1000031 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Mass spectrometer instrument make and model.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology. If multiple instruments, return a list.\n  Common instruments (name → accession):\n    Orbitrap Astral → MS:1003356\n    Orbitrap Exploris 480 → MS:1003028\n    Orbitrap Eclipse → MS:1003349\n    Orbitrap Fusion Lumos → MS:1002732\n    Orbitrap Fusion → MS:1002416\n    Q Exactive HF-X → MS:1002877\n    Q Exactive HF → MS:1002523\n    Q Exactive Plus → MS:1002634\n    Q Exactive → MS:1001911\n    LTQ Orbitrap Velos → MS:1001742\n    LTQ Orbitrap XL → MS:1000556\n    timsTOF → MS:1003005\n    timsTOF Pro → MS:1003230\n    TripleTOF 6600 → MS:1002583\n  Format: NT=<instrument name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: proteomics data acquisition method\nsdrf_column: comment[proteomics data acquisition method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000659\ndescription: Mass spectrometry data acquisition method.\ninstructions: For XL-MS this is nearly always DDA. Use:\n  NT=data-dependent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000041 — for DDA/shotgun\n  NT=data-independent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000449 — for DIA (rare in XL-MS)\n  NT=targeted acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000450 — for SRM/PRM\n  Default to DDA if not stated.\n\nterm: dissociation method\nsdrf_column: comment[dissociation method]\nontology: MS:1000044 (PSI-MS children)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nCSE1L and NUP37 proximity labeling in MCF10A cells expressing chimeric ErbB1:ErbB2 receptors\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThis project uses an MCF10A clone (B2B1) expressing chimeric synthetic receptors for ErbB1 (EGFR) and ErbB2 (HER2) along with BirA*-fused inducible alleles of CSE1L or NUP37.  Cells were cultured with reconstituted basement membrane, BirA* alleles induced with doxycycline, ErbB receptors dimerized with a synthetic small molecule, and biotinylation was performed for 24 hours.  Lysates underwent a two-step biotin affinity purification before gel electrophoresis and mass spectrometry on selected gel fragments.\n\n[METHODS]\nFour 15-cm plates of B2B1 cells stably expressing inducible BirA*-CSE1L or BirA*-NUP37 were prepared as overlay cultures, induced with 1 µg/ml doxycycline (Sigma, D9891) on Day 5, heterodimerized with 0.5 µM AP21967 (Takara, 635057) on Day 6, and labeled with 50 µM biotin (Sigma, B4639) on Day 7 for 24 hours before lysis in RIPA buffer (50 mM Tris-HCl [pH 7.5], 150 mM NaCl, 1% Triton X-100, 0.5% sodium deoxycholate, 0.1% sodium dodecyl sulfate, 5 mM EDTA) plus protease and phosphatase inhibitors (10 µg/ml aprotinin, 10 µg/ml leupeptin, 1 µg/ml pepstatin, 1 mM PMSF, 200 µM Na3VO4).  Clarified lysate (3 ml) was mixed with 8 ml IAP buffer (50 mM MOPS [pH 7.2], 10 mM sodium phosphate [pH 7.4], 50 mM NaCl) and immunoprecipitated with 500 µl anti-biotin agarose (ImmuneChem, ICP0615) overnight at 4ºC.  Immunoprecipitates were washed four times with PBS plus protease and phosphatase inhibitors and eluted with 0.15% trifluoroacetic acid followed by re-equilibration to pH 7 with 1 M NaOH.  The eluate was re-purified with 50 µl streptavidin magnetic beads (Thermo Fisher, PI88816) for two hours at room temperature, washed three times with TBS + 0.1% Tween 20 plus protease and phosphatase inhibitors, and boiled in Laemmli sample buffer.  Samples were electrophoresed on a 10% polyacrylamide gel and fragments excised to avoid nonspecific bands at 57, 41, 27, and 16 kDa before mass spectrometry.  Gel fragments were transferred to a siliconized tube, washed in 200 µl 50% methanol, dehydrated in acetonitrile, rehydrated in 30 µl 10 mM dithiolthreitol in 0.1 M ammonium bicarbonate, and reduced at room temperature for 0.5 h.  Samples were alkylated in 30 µl 50 mM iodoacetamide in 0.1 M ammonium bicarbonate at room temperature for 30 minutes, dehydrated in 100 µl acetonitrile, rehydrated in 100 µl 0.1 M ammonium bicarbonate, and then dehydrated again in 100 µL acetonitrile before drying completely by vacuum centrifugation.  Gel fragments were rehydrated in 20 ng/µl trypsin in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate on ice for 30 minutes.  Excess enzyme solution was removed, 20 µl 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate added, and the sample was digested overnight at 37ºC.  Digested peptides were extracted in 100 µl 50% acetonitrile and 5% formic acid and evaporated to 15 µl for MS analysis on a Thermo Electron Q Exactive HFX mass spectrometer system with an Easy Spray ion source connected to a Thermo 75 µm x 15 cm C18 Easy Spray column.  A 4 µl or 7 µl equivalent of the extract was injected and the peptides eluted from the column by an acetonitrile/0.1 M acetic acid gradient at a flow rate of 0.3 µl/minute over one hour.  The nanospray ion source was operated at 1.9 kV.  The digest was analyzed by acquiring a full scan mass spectrum to determine peptide molecular weights followed by product ion spectra (20 HCD) to determine amino acid sequence in sequential scans.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
