2026-06-30 15:30:08,282 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-e93bdfc6-921d-4f93-a21c-3f33f54c233e', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to sample preparation and digestion.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: cleavage agent details\nsdrf_column: comment[cleavage agent details]\nontology: MS:1001044 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Enzyme or chemical used for protein digestion.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology terms. Multiple enzymes are common in XL-MS.\n  If multiple, return a list.\n  Common terms:\n    Trypsin (MS:1001251) — cleaves K,R (not before P)\n    Lys-C (MS:1001309) — cleaves K\n    Lys-C/P (MS:1001309) — cleaves K including before P\n    Asp-N (MS:1001303) — cleaves N-terminal to D\n    Glu-C (MS:1001917) — cleaves C-terminal to E, D\n    Chymotrypsin (MS:1001306) — cleaves F,Y,W,L\n    No cleavage (MS:1001955)\n    Unspecific cleavage (MS:1001956)\n  Format each as: NT=<enzyme name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: label\nsdrf_column: comment[label]\nontology: PRIDE:0000514, MS\ndescription: Labeling strategy used for quantification.\ninstructions: Most XL-MS is label-free. Use:\n  NT=label free sample;AC=MS:1002038 — for label-free\n  NT=SILAC;AC=MS:1002038 — for SILAC\n  NT=iTRAQ4plex;AC=PRIDE:0000114 — for iTRAQ 4-plex\n  NT=TMT6plex;AC=PRIDE:0000116 — for TMT\n  If not stated, assume label free.\n\nterm: alkylation reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[alkylation reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000598\ndescription: Chemical reagent used for cysteine alkylation (typically before digestion).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Common: iodoacetamide (IAA), iodoacetic acid,\n  N-ethylmaleimide (NEM), acrylamide. If iodoacetamide, use "iodoacetamide".\n\nterm: fractionation method\nsdrf_column: comment[fractionation method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000550\ndescription: Peptide fractionation method used before LC-MS/MS (distinct from crosslink\n  enrichment — this is standard proteomics fractionation).\ninstructions: Return null if no pre-LC fractionation. Examples: "high-pH reversed-phase\n  chromatography", "basic reversed-phase LC", "SCX fractionation", "HILIC".\n\nterm: modification parameters\nsdrf_column: comment[modification parameters]\nontology: MS:1001055, Unimod, PSI-MOD\ndescription: Post-translational modifications searched in the database search.\noutput_format: List of modifications, each formatted as:\n  NT=<mod name>;AC=<Unimod accession>;TA=<target amino acid>;MT=<Fixed or Variable>\ninstructions: Always include carbamidomethyl cysteine if IAA alkylation was used:\n  NT=Carbamidomethyl;AC=UNIMOD:4;TA=C;MT=Fixed\n  Always include oxidation of methionine if searched:\n  NT=Oxidation;AC=UNIMOD:21;TA=M;MT=Variable\n  Include crosslinker mono-link (dead-end) modifications if mentioned.\n  Return a list even if only one modification.\n\nterm: fraction identifier\nsdrf_column: comment[fraction identifier]\nontology: MS:1000858\ndescription: Fraction number for fractionated samples.\ninstructions: Describe whether the dataset uses fractionation and what the fractionation\n  scheme is (e.g. "12 SEC fractions per replicate", "no fractionation").\n  The actual integer values are assigned per-file; describe the scheme here.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nFor comment[modification parameters], "value" should be a list of formatted strings.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nCSE1L and NUP37 proximity labeling in MCF10A cells expressing chimeric ErbB1:ErbB2 receptors\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThis project uses an MCF10A clone (B2B1) expressing chimeric synthetic receptors for ErbB1 (EGFR) and ErbB2 (HER2) along with BirA*-fused inducible alleles of CSE1L or NUP37.  Cells were cultured with reconstituted basement membrane, BirA* alleles induced with doxycycline, ErbB receptors dimerized with a synthetic small molecule, and biotinylation was performed for 24 hours.  Lysates underwent a two-step biotin affinity purification before gel electrophoresis and mass spectrometry on selected gel fragments.\n\n[METHODS]\nFour 15-cm plates of B2B1 cells stably expressing inducible BirA*-CSE1L or BirA*-NUP37 were prepared as overlay cultures, induced with 1 µg/ml doxycycline (Sigma, D9891) on Day 5, heterodimerized with 0.5 µM AP21967 (Takara, 635057) on Day 6, and labeled with 50 µM biotin (Sigma, B4639) on Day 7 for 24 hours before lysis in RIPA buffer (50 mM Tris-HCl [pH 7.5], 150 mM NaCl, 1% Triton X-100, 0.5% sodium deoxycholate, 0.1% sodium dodecyl sulfate, 5 mM EDTA) plus protease and phosphatase inhibitors (10 µg/ml aprotinin, 10 µg/ml leupeptin, 1 µg/ml pepstatin, 1 mM PMSF, 200 µM Na3VO4).  Clarified lysate (3 ml) was mixed with 8 ml IAP buffer (50 mM MOPS [pH 7.2], 10 mM sodium phosphate [pH 7.4], 50 mM NaCl) and immunoprecipitated with 500 µl anti-biotin agarose (ImmuneChem, ICP0615) overnight at 4ºC.  Immunoprecipitates were washed four times with PBS plus protease and phosphatase inhibitors and eluted with 0.15% trifluoroacetic acid followed by re-equilibration to pH 7 with 1 M NaOH.  The eluate was re-purified with 50 µl streptavidin magnetic beads (Thermo Fisher, PI88816) for two hours at room temperature, washed three times with TBS + 0.1% Tween 20 plus protease and phosphatase inhibitors, and boiled in Laemmli sample buffer.  Samples were electrophoresed on a 10% polyacrylamide gel and fragments excised to avoid nonspecific bands at 57, 41, 27, and 16 kDa before mass spectrometry.  Gel fragments were transferred to a siliconized tube, washed in 200 µl 50% methanol, dehydrated in acetonitrile, rehydrated in 30 µl 10 mM dithiolthreitol in 0.1 M ammonium bicarbonate, and reduced at room temperature for 0.5 h.  Samples were alkylated in 30 µl 50 mM iodoacetamide in 0.1 M ammonium bicarbonate at room temperature for 30 minutes, dehydrated in 100 µl acetonitrile, rehydrated in 100 µl 0.1 M ammonium bicarbonate, and then dehydrated again in 100 µL acetonitrile before drying completely by vacuum centrifugation.  Gel fragments were rehydrated in 20 ng/µl trypsin in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate on ice for 30 minutes.  Excess enzyme solution was removed, 20 µl 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate added, and the sample was digested overnight at 37ºC.  Digested peptides were extracted in 100 µl 50% acetonitrile and 5% formic acid and evaporated to 15 µl for MS analysis on a Thermo Electron Q Exactive HFX mass spectrometer system with an Easy Spray ion source connected to a Thermo 75 µm x 15 cm C18 Easy Spray column.  A 4 µl or 7 µl equivalent of the extract was injected and the peptides eluted from the column by an acetonitrile/0.1 M acetic acid gradient at a flow rate of 0.3 µl/minute over one hour.  The nanospray ion source was operated at 1.9 kV.  The digest was analyzed by acquiring a full scan mass spectrum to determine peptide molecular weights followed by product ion spectra (20 HCD) to determine amino acid sequence in sequential scans.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
